## Reproduced defect: guarded terminal update conflicts with its own first write

A single, sequential `commitItemMutations` update can change scalar fields and then reject its own close transition with `upsert_match_changed`. No intervening writer is required. The item remains open with the scalar edit persisted, while the transaction journal reports `compensated`.

### Tested revision and scope
- Reproduced against the built public SDK from source `df5bf4aa4db17b7743b4f6bfddb32098f2cbd521`, Node 24.22.1, in a fresh synthetic non-Git tracker.
- Current default `dc10f63f671456b5d50d31d802126656fc22896c` was checked: `src/sdk/item-transaction.ts`, `src/sdk/lifecycle/update.ts`, and `src/core/store/item-mutation-precondition.ts` are byte-identical to the tested revision.
- No claim about an independently installed npm artifact, live provider behavior, concurrency, crash recovery, or whole-release certification.

### Minimal reproduction
Using the tested SDK exports (initialize `pmRoot` in a fresh temporary directory):

```js
const pm = new PmClient({
  cwd: root, pmRoot, author: "synthetic-review", noExtensions: true
});
await pm.init("fixture");
const { item } = await pm.create({
  title: "Synthetic guarded terminal transition"
});
await commitItemMutations({
  pmRoot, author: "synthetic-review", transactionId: "synthetic-terminal",
  mutations: [{
    op: "update", id: item.id,
    options: {
      title: "Synthetic changed title",
      status: "closed",
      closeReason: "Synthetic completion"
    },
    match: { key: "updated_at", value: item.updated_at }
  }]
});
```

Actual:
1. Throws `PmCliError`, exit 4, context code `upsert_match_changed`.
2. Subsequent complete list shows title `Synthetic changed title`, status `open`, and a newer `updated_at`.
3. Durable history contains an `update` replacing title and `updated_at`; no close event or scalar restoration.
4. `transactions/sdk/synthetic-terminal.json` has `status: "compensated"`, empty `startedStepIds`, `completedStepIds`, `results`, and `compensationData`.

This is not a demand for atomic rollback across independent providers. It is a self-induced guard failure within one SDK mutation, accompanied by an inaccurate fully-compensated-looking journal state.

### Contract and cause
The [guard contract](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/dc10f63f671456b5d50d31d802126656fc22896c/src/sdk/item-transaction.ts#L254-L268) rechecks the selected key against the locked target before writing. [Canonical bulk acceptance](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/dc10f63f671456b5d50d31d802126656fc22896c/.agents/pm/features/pm-gh1456.toon) records guarded upserts and compensated replay; original delivery was [PR #1479](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/pull/1479).

[The update transaction](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/dc10f63f671456b5d50d31d802126656fc22896c/src/sdk/item-transaction.ts#L606-L623) scopes the precondition around the entire update. [Terminal routing](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/dc10f63f671456b5d50d31d802126656fc22896c/src/sdk/lifecycle/update.ts#L2069-L2095) first updates scalar fields, advancing `updated_at`, then closes; the same precondition remains active for the second mutation. This explanation is static analysis consistent with the sequential reproduction.

### Consumer impact
[pm-ado PR #46](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/pull/46) already splits a guarded scalar update and explicit close into separate SDK transaction steps. Its actual [pm-ado-sdk-match-close-1025 record](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/blob/ebb1b3fd24726362198394ef9a980fdf53395675/.agents/pm/issues/pm-ado-sdk-match-close-1025.toon) identifies this pinned-2026.10.23 limitation; that consumer evidence is separate from the current-source reproduction above. Thin backends should not each implement lifecycle workarounds.

### Acceptance / regression criteria
- A matching timestamp guard with scalar edits plus a terminal transition succeeds without falsely detecting the operation's own writes.
- A genuinely stale starting guard refuses before any item/history mutation.
- Preserve intended lock-held safety across internal lifecycle steps; simply disabling subsequent validation must not weaken writer safety.
- If a step partially mutates before failing, journal/history and surfaced recovery state truthfully describe or compensate those changes; do not report compensated while leaving an unreported partial scalar change.
- Add a sequential SDK regression, lifecycle/history assertions, and a regression for compensation receipts. Reuse the shared SDK fix in consumers when their pinned host supports it.

All-status exact-error/self-conflict/terminal-precondition searches and the canonical bulk PM record were checked; no separate existing issue for this reproduction was found. No implementation changes made.

Independent intake verification: reproduced on Node26.12.1 using the current checkout build in an isolated non-Git fixture with both PM roots isolated and telemetry/Sentry disabled. upsert_match_changed exit4 leaves the edited title on an open item with a newer updated_at; the transaction journal says compensated with empty started/completed step sets. This is reproduction evidence, not a fix or published-artifact claim.
